# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# Next's static export content-hashes everything under /_next/static, so those
# assets can be cached indefinitely. The HTML above them must not be.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public,max-age=31536000,immutable

# Security headers. None of these close a live vulnerability: the site is a
# static export with no server, no forms posting anywhere and no auth, and the
# origin answers 405 to every method but GET. They are defence in depth for the
# browser, and they cost nothing to serve.
#
# Comments stay at the top level. A `#` line indented inside a rule block is not
# reliably treated as a comment by the _headers parser, and one malformed line
# there can drop the whole block — silently, with no build error.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security — only reach this origin over https, upgraded in the
# browser before the request leaves the machine. Without it the first visit to
# the bare domain is one plaintext round trip that a network attacker can
# rewrite. Requires Cloudflare's "Always Use HTTPS" to be on: a browser that has
# only ever seen http never receives this header at all.
#
# X-Frame-Options — stops the page being framed and passed off as someone
# else's. This matters more here than on a brochure site: these tools take
# pasted input, and a framed clone that looks like the real decoder is a
# plausible way to harvest what someone pastes into it.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy — severs window.opener so a cross-origin window
# cannot retain a handle on this one.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options — honour the declared content-type rather than sniffing
# it.
#
# Referrer-Policy — send only the origin to other sites, the full url to our
# own. Tool state lives in the page rather than the query string, but this keeps
# paths out of the Referer header on any outbound link regardless.
#
# No CSP, deliberately. Next injects inline bootstrap scripts into the exported
# HTML, and static hosting has no per-request server to mint a nonce — so a
# policy means hashing those by hand and re-hashing on every build. Add one if a
# third-party script is ever introduced; today there are none, which is the
# property that actually keeps pasted input on the machine it was typed on.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
